/// bcrypt — Adaptive Password Hashing Function (Niels Provos, 1999).
///
/// bcrypt is a password hashing function designed to be slow and adaptable,
/// making brute-force attacks expensive. It is based on the Blowfish cipher
/// with a modified key schedule called "EksBlowfishSetup" (Expensive Key
/// Schedule Blowfish Setup) that runs the key schedule `2^cost` times.
///
/// ## Algorithm
///
/// ```text
/// 1. EksBlowfishSetup(cost, salt, password):
///    - Initialise Blowfish P-array and S-boxes with pi digits.
///    - XOR the password into the P-array (cyclically).
///    - Encrypt the all-zero block, replacing P and S entries with the
///      ciphertext output, alternating left and right halves.
///    - Repeat the entire key schedule expansion 2^cost times.
///
/// 2. bcrypt(password, cost, salt):
///    - Run EksBlowfishSetup.
///    - Encrypt the 24-byte string "OrpheanBeholderScryDoubt" 64 times.
///    - Output: $2b$<cost>$<base64(salt)><base64(hash[:23])>
/// ```
///
/// ## Cost parameter
///
/// The cost parameter controls how many iterations the key schedule runs:
/// `2^cost` rounds. Valid range is 4–31. OWASP recommends cost ≥ 12 for
/// production use (≈250ms on modern hardware).
///
/// ## Salt
///
/// The salt must be exactly 16 bytes (128 bits) of cryptographically random
/// data. Use the `crypto/csprng` module to generate it.
///
/// ## Output format
///
/// The hash string follows the modular crypt format:
/// ```text
/// $2b$<cost>$<22-char-base64-salt><31-char-base64-hash>
/// ```
/// Total length: 60 characters.
///
/// ## Security notes
///
/// - bcrypt has a 72-byte password limit. Passwords longer than 72 bytes
///   are silently truncated. For longer passwords, pre-hash with SHA-256
///   (but be aware of null-byte issues; use `$2b$` with base64-encoded
///   pre-hash if needed).
///
/// - MoonBit has a garbage collector; password bytes may persist in memory
///   until GC collects them. This is a platform limitation.
///
/// - This implementation has NOT been audited by a third-party security firm.
///
/// ## References
///
/// - N. Provos, D. Mazières. "A Future-Adaptable Password Scheme."
///   USENIX Annual Technical Conference, FREENIX Track, 1999.
/// - bcrypt specification: https://github.com/kelektiv/node.bcrypt.js/blob/master/lib/bcrypt/bcrypt.c
///
/// `proof-enabled` is `false`; correctness is backed by test vectors.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Blowfish constants (fractional parts of π)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

///|
/// Initial P-array: 18 32-bit words from the fractional part of π.
/// These are the standard Blowfish initialization constants.
